WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.560
Take away nerve growth factor, NGF, from a young sympathetic neuron in a dish.

2
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.060
It dies within a day or two.

3
00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.000
Now add a drug that stops it making new proteins.

4
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.020
The death is delayed.

5
00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:22.560
That is strange, because a starving cell should run down faster.

6
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.180
Instead, the neuron has to make something in order to die.

7
00:00:27.983 --> 00:00:32.263
So dying is a program, and a worm revealed its parts.

8
00:00:33.203 --> 00:00:38.783
In a transparent worm, the same named cells die on schedule in every animal.

9
00:00:39.743 --> 00:00:45.083
Two genes, ced-3 and ced-4, are needed for these deaths.

10
00:00:45.783 --> 00:00:47.263
Without them, the cells live.

11
00:00:48.243 --> 00:00:53.263
Lose a third gene, ced-9, and cells that should live die.

12
00:00:53.263 --> 00:00:58.903
Remove ced-3 or ced-4 as well, and those deaths stop.

13
00:00:59.783 --> 00:01:02.403
Cloning connected the worm to mammals.

14
00:01:03.703 --> 00:01:10.043
ced-3 encodes a protein similar to a protease, of the family later named caspases.

15
00:01:11.463 --> 00:01:15.623
ced-4 is related to a mammalian protein, Apaf-1.

16
00:01:17.203 --> 00:01:23.023
ced-9 encodes a relative of Bcl-2, a protein that keeps cells alive.

17
00:01:23.963 --> 00:01:25.283
The match is imperfect.

18
00:01:25.283 --> 00:01:28.623
The worm has no clear counterpart of Bax.

19
00:01:29.417 --> 00:01:32.337
Now return to the neuron without NGF.

20
00:01:33.297 --> 00:01:34.817
Its survival signal falls.

21
00:01:35.597 --> 00:01:40.277
A stress pathway turns on, and a protein called c-Jun rises.

22
00:01:41.557 --> 00:01:44.217
Pro-death relatives of Bcl-2 are made.

23
00:01:45.077 --> 00:01:48.097
This is the new gene expression that the drug blocked.

24
00:01:49.317 --> 00:01:52.677
Bax is needed for cytochrome c to leave the mitochondria.

25
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:58.120
In the cytoplasm, cytochrome c binds Apaf-1.

26
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.000
Apaf-1 assembles into a wheel called the apoptosome.

27
00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:09.540
The apoptosome activates caspase-9, which cuts and activates caspase-3.

28
00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.300
Executioner caspases dismantle the cell.

29
00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.940
They also expose an "eat me" signal on its surface.

30
00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:20.863
Is that the only step NGF controls?

31
00:02:22.463 --> 00:02:26.683
Researchers injected cytochrome c into neurons kept in NGF.

32
00:02:27.403 --> 00:02:28.503
The neurons survived.

33
00:02:29.643 --> 00:02:36.403
Neurons deprived of NGF for about 15 to 20 hours died after the injection, and a

34
00:02:36.403 --> 00:02:38.643
caspase inhibitor prevented their death.

35
00:02:39.303 --> 00:02:43.803
So NGF holds the death program back at more than one step.

36
00:02:43.803 --> 00:02:48.543
Death is a program, held back at more than one step.
